  "full_response": "It's important to clarify that **Antarctica is not expected to completely melt by the end of the year—or even within this century**. While climate change is causing significant ice loss in Antarctica (and Greenland), a full melt would take **thousands of years** under current projections, even in high-emission scenarios.\n\nHowever, **sea level rise from Antarctic ice melt is a serious long-term threat**, and countries are taking steps to prepare for its impacts. Here’s how nations are responding:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Mitigation (Reducing Emissions to Slow Melting)**\nThe primary focus is on **limiting global warming** to slow Antarctic ice loss:\n- **Paris Agreement (2015):** 196 countries committed to keeping global warming \"well below\" 2°C (ideally 1.5°C) to avoid catastrophic ice sheet collapse.\n- **Net-Zero Pledges:** Over 130 countries (including the U.S., EU, China, and India) have set net-zero emissions targets by 2050–2070.\n- **Renewable Energy Expansion:** Solar, wind, and hydroelectric power are being scaled up to replace fossil fuels.\n- **Carbon Pricing & Policies:** Many nations (e.g., Canada, Sweden, the EU) have implemented carbon taxes or cap-and-trade systems.\n\n**Why this matters for Antarctica:**\n- The **West Antarctic Ice Sheet (WAIS)** is particularly vulnerable to warming. If global temperatures rise **2–3°C above pre-industrial levels**, scientists warn of **irreversible collapse**, leading to **several meters of sea level rise over centuries**.\n- The **Thwaites Glacier (\"Doomsday Glacier\")** is already destabilizing, contributing to **4% of global sea level rise annually**.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Adaptation (Preparing for Sea Level Rise)**\nEven with mitigation, some sea level rise is **locked in** due to past emissions. Countries are preparing for **0.3–1 meter (1–3 feet) of rise by 2100** (IPCC projections) and planning for **worse-case scenarios (2+ meters)**.\n\n#### **A. Coastal Defense & Infrastructure**\n- **The Netherlands:** A global leader in flood protection, with **dikes, storm surge barriers (e.g., Maeslantkering), and \"Room for the River\" projects** to manage rising waters.\n- **UK & Europe",
